Consider the Edwards–Anderson short-range spin glass on the lattice $\mathbb{Z}^d$ (with $d\ge 2$), defined by the formal Hamiltonian
$$H(\sigma) \;=\; -\sum_{\langle x,y\rangle} J_{xy}\,\sigma_x \sigma_y,$$
where $\sigma_x \in \{-1,+1\}$ are Ising spins on the sites of $\mathbb{Z}^d$, the sum is over nearest-neighbor pairs, and the couplings $\{J_{xy}\}$ are i.i.d. random variables with a symmetric continuous distribution (e.g. standard Gaussian). For each realization of the couplings, let $\langle\,\cdot\,\rangle_\beta$ denote any infinite-volume Gibbs state at inverse temperature $\beta$ obtained as a thermodynamic limit of finite-volume Gibbs measures with arbitrary boundary conditions.

Assume that for sufficiently large $\beta$ a low-temperature "spin glass phase" exists (i.e. the free energy is non-analytic in $\beta$ and the system fails to be a single pure paramagnetic state). Provide a mathematically and physically correct definition of an order parameter for this phase, that is, a quantity (or family of quantities) constructed from the Gibbs measures, which:

(a) is well-defined as a function of the disorder $\{J_{xy}\}$ in the infinite-volume limit;
(b) is zero in the high-temperature (paramagnetic) phase and nonzero in the conjectured low-temperature phase;
(c) faithfully encodes the symmetry that is broken in the low-temperature phase, including a description of the pure-state decomposition of the Gibbs measure(s);
(d) reduces, in mean-field (Sherrington–Kirkpatrick) limits where appropriate, to a recognized order-parameter structure, while correctly accounting for the differences between mean-field replica-symmetry-breaking pictures and any genuine short-range structure.

Determine such an order parameter, or prove that no single scalar order parameter suffices and characterize the minimal structure (e.g. a metastate, an overlap distribution, or a measure on pure states) that does.

